WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:05.559
The Chemistry Behind Liquid Products. This is module 1 of the course.

2
00:00:06.006 --> 00:00:11.776
Three tools first: which surfactant does which job, how pH works, and how to keep germs out.

3
00:00:12.776 --> 00:00:16.577
Look at this bucket. We're stirring a surfactant into water, gently.

4
00:00:17.055 --> 00:00:21.788
Each surfactant molecule has a head that loves water and a tail that loves oil.

5
00:00:22.222 --> 00:00:29.043
The tails gather into tiny balls called micelles. Grease slips inside, and the rinse water carries it away.

6
00:00:30.043 --> 00:00:32.781
On this shelf they're grouped by the charge on the head.

7
00:00:33.261 --> 00:00:43.145
Anionics like SLES clean and foam. CAPB makes them milder. Nonionics cut grease. Cationics disinfect, condition hair and soften fabric.

8
00:00:44.145 --> 00:00:47.968
This milky liquid is fabric softener, a cationic product.

9
00:00:48.353 --> 00:00:54.678
Anionic and cationic surfactants attract each other and form a sticky solid that does neither job.

10
00:00:55.044 --> 00:00:58.350
That's why softener goes in the rinse, not with the detergent.

11
00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:09.180
Watch the scale as we scoop in SLES paste. It's commonly about 70% active, so 100 g holds about 70 g of real surfactant.

12
00:01:09.521 --> 00:01:16.423
CAPB is usually around 30% active. Always read your supplier's data sheet, because the figure changes.

13
00:01:17.423 --> 00:01:22.703
Here's Bisi's sum. Active matter is the percentage you use times its strength, added up.

14
00:01:23.166 --> 00:01:30.549
Her formula is 12.3% active, 615 g of real surfactant in 5 kg.

15
00:01:30.949 --> 00:01:40.146
When her new SLES was only 60% active, she used 875 g instead of 750 g to keep the same strength.

16
00:01:41.146 --> 00:01:50.546
This strip reads pH, from 0 to 14. Water is 7. Below is acidic, above is alkaline, and each step is ten times stronger.

17
00:01:50.963 --> 00:01:56.284
Dip, wait the time on the pack, and compare the colour in daylight. Test before you add dye.

18
00:01:57.284 --> 00:02:01.136
Before making any adjuster solution, put on gloves and goggles.

19
00:02:01.522 --> 00:02:10.692
Add acid or caustic slowly to water, never water onto the chemical, because heat and splashing can result. Label every adjuster bottle.

20
00:02:11.692 --> 00:02:22.421
Amaka weighed a 200 g sample, 1% of her batch, and added citric solution in drops until it read pH 6. That took 0.9 g.

21
00:02:22.834 --> 00:02:30.586
Times 100 gives 90 g. She added 72 g first, tested, then the rest slowly, so she never overshot.

22
00:02:31.586 --> 00:02:37.314
When you test products at home, keep the toilet cleaner and the bleach apart, just like these two bottles.

23
00:02:38.314 --> 00:02:45.395
Liquid soap is mostly water, and water feeds germs. So use treated water, or water boiled and cooled covered.

24
00:02:45.750 --> 00:02:52.506
Wash drums and paddles, rinse, wipe with 70% alcohol and let them dry. Never use them for food.

25
00:02:53.506 --> 00:03:02.564
A preservative stops germs that get in later. Hauwa checked her pH was in the supplier's range, then weighed 200 g on a digital scale.

26
00:03:03.006 --> 00:03:10.652
Add it below about 40 degrees Celsius, because heat can damage some preservatives. And avoid formalin: it is toxic.

27
00:03:11.652 --> 00:03:16.012
Now fill into new or sanitised bottles, then cap and label quickly.

28
00:03:17.012 --> 00:03:21.564
Keep two bottles from every batch, one at room temperature, one somewhere warm.

29
00:03:22.035 --> 00:03:28.232
Check them weekly for four weeks, then monthly: smell, colour, clarity, thickness, pH and swelling.

30
00:03:29.232 --> 00:03:38.826
Read the Safety Data Sheet for every chemical. Wear nitrile gloves and goggles, ventilate, and keep an eyewash bottle of clean water nearby, like this one.

31
00:03:39.826 --> 00:03:48.181
Now go through the lessons below. Build your raw material register, map the pH of five products at home, and set up your clean corner.

